\section{Two Modes and One Core Language}

Bidirectional typing splits the job of the checker into two judgments. In $\Gamma\vdash e\Rightarrow A$, the expression synthesizes its type; in $\Gamma\vdash e\Leftarrow A$, a type already in hand is checked against the expression. Each direction can also return an elaborated core term. A variable synthesizes by lookup, and an application synthesizes by first discovering an arrow at its head and then checking its argument against the domain. An unannotated lambda is easier to check: the domain supplies the binder's type, and the codomain is the expectation for its body. A lambda in a position with no expected arrow may need an ascription even though the corresponding declarative calculus permits some typing for it.

Benjamin C. Pierce and David N. Turner's \emph{Local Type Inference} recovers information from adjacent syntax, using expected types for abstractions and local solving for polymorphic applications rather than whole-program inference. One benefit of this design is a predictable source annotation policy. It need not reproduce their full system: the following example uses only checking at an expected arrow, synthesis of primitive types, and a checked ascription. The core language can use fully annotated binders even when the source omits them.

\section{Worked Elaboration: Follow the Expected Type}

Suppose $\mathsf{atZero} : (\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Bool})\to\mathsf{Bool}$ applies its callback to zero. Furthermore, let $(<)$ be the integer comparison and $(+)$ be integer addition. For the source term $\mathsf{atZero}(\lambda x.\,x<1)$, the key steps are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Synthesize the type of $\mathsf{atZero}$. Its domain becomes the expectation $\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Bool}$ for the lambda.
  \item Bind $x : \mathsf{Int}$ and check the body against $\mathsf{Bool}$. The comparison synthesizes $\mathsf{Bool}$ after its operands check against $\mathsf{Int}$.
  \item Emit an application with the annotated lambda $\lambda(x : \mathsf{Int}).\,x<1$ and the result type $\mathsf{Bool}$.
\end{enumerate}
Replacing the body by $x+1$ leaves the binder justified in exactly the same way. Only the last comparison for the body now fails: it produces $\mathsf{Int}$, but the return type of the callback requires $\mathsf{Bool}$. The best primary span is the body, with a note pointing to the expected callback signature. By contrast, for $\lambda x.\,x<1$ at a synthesis-only top level, this algorithm asks for a type rather than reporting an inconsistency. An ascription
\[
  (\lambda x.\,x<1)\ :\ \mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Bool}
\]
checks the inner expression and lets the outer expression synthesize the ascribed type.

\section{Keep Reasons Alongside Solutions}

Duggan and Bent's \emph{Explaining Type Inference} modifies Hindley--Milner unification to record the reasons behind inferred types. The same principle applies to mixed-direction checking. Keep an origin for each expected type or solver obligation: an explicit annotation, a callee domain, a pattern constructor, or an instantiation of a quantified declaration. When decomposing a comparison, extend the origin with the path to the failing component, such as ``callback result.'' For a metavariable, keep the reason for its assignment separately from the mutable pointer used to retrieve its current solution. Path compression must not erase the only route from a failure to the source occurrence that first fixed the type.

When recovery is needed, use a distinct error marker and suppress failures that depend only on it; do not treat the marker as an implementable cast or install a recovered core term in the build. A successful result, in contrast, should pass a core recheck with every inserted coercion and type argument made explicit according to the core's rules. Scope is part of this invariant: an inference metavariable created outside a binder must not be solved with a rigid variable introduced inside it. Higher-rank checking must therefore distinguish entering a quantifier (with a fresh rigid variable) from using a polymorphic value (with an instantiation).

An origin explains a contradiction but does not identify the programmer's intended repair. Saying ``expected Boolean because of this callback parameter'' is stronger evidence than suggesting a conversion whose semantics are unknown. The mode split is also not the ``bidirectional compiler'' idea in Markdown Dossier Chapter~06, which explores translation between representations in both directions; both typing modes here elaborate toward the same core.
