\section{Checking Record Fields at Their Own Expected Types}

An expected product type can carry several independent checking
obligations. Consider a core with labeled records, arrows, and two
primitive types, but no record subtyping, defaults, or field
overloading. A surface record literal may contain unannotated
lambdas, so synthesizing all its field types and comparing the
finished record is too restrictive. Instead take a record literal
and an expected record type, verify that their field names agree,
then check each initializer with the type of that field. Field
initializers run in source order and cannot refer to sibling fields.
Return either the annotated record core or a diagnostic carrying
both the initializer and the origin of the field expectation.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input literal fields $F$, required record type $R$, origin $o$
  \Output checked core record or diagnostic
  \If{$F$ has duplicate labels}
    \State \Return duplicate-label diagnostic
  \EndIf
  \If{$\Call{Labels}{F}\ne\Call{Labels}{R}$}
    \State \Return missing or extra field diagnostic
  \EndIf
  \State $C\gets$ empty list
  \ForAll{$(l,e)$ in $F$ in source order}
    \Comment{Do not sort effectful fields}
    \State $p\gets\Call{ExtendOrigin}{o,l}$
    \Comment{Keep the expected field path}
    \State $c\gets\Call{Check}{e,R[l],p}$ or report failure
    \State append $(l,c)$ to $C$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\Call{Record}{C}$
\end{sudocode}

Suppose a parameter has type
$\{\mathsf{start}:\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Int},
\mathsf{ready}:\mathsf{Bool}\}$. Pass a record that lists
\texttt{ready = true} before \texttt{start = lambda n. n + 2}.
The first check yields a Boolean constant. The second introduces
$n:\mathsf{Int}$ and checks the sum at $\mathsf{Int}$. The
resulting core preserves the field order and has an explicit binder
type; selecting its \texttt{start} field and calling it at $5$
returns $7$. If \texttt{ready} is written as $1$, report that
initializer's span and the path \texttt{parameter.ready}. Asking
for a lambda annotation in this second input would obscure the
actual failure.

The invariant is that lookup by label chooses the type, while the
list order determines evaluation. Thus two field permutations can
have the same static type without having the same effects.
Detect duplicate labels before building a map that would silently
drop an initializer. With hashed labels, checking the record shell
costs expected linear time aside from recursive checks. For a
computed record expression rather than a literal, use the general
synthesis-and-comparison rule; evaluation is not a source of
static field expectations.
